{"t":1791127325,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"f2fc4a00-9c9a-484d-aa0f-d1d15f883047","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-04T14:12:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 041412\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n1012 AM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe main shield of rainfall looks to be staying south of the\nI-95 metros. However, areas of drizzle will continue to be an\nissue for much of the region, with widespread low stratus in\nplace.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A frontal system nearby will maintain rain chances today\n  with below average temperatures.\n\n- 2) Frost and freezing temperatures are possible to the west of\n  the Blue Ridge early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A frontal system nearby will maintain rain\nchances today with below average temperatures.\n\nA frontal boundary remains stalled across the coastal Carolinas\nback down across the southern U.S. Despite the local area being\nin a stable post-frontal air mass, the atmosphere remains rather\nmoist per the 12Z IAD sounding (~1.25 inch precipitable water\nvalue). This sounding showed much of the moisture in the surface\nto 700-mb layer, with immense mid-level dry air above. In the \nnet, this is supporting a thick area of low stratus across the \nentire region. This is being accompanied by patchy areas of \ndrizzle, although some more concentrated shower activity has \ndeveloped near I-64. Expect this to track northeastward in time \nwhich would move this activity into southern Maryland within the\nnext couple hours. Some additional precipitation may develop \nfurther to the north as well.\n\nA persistent northeast wind should keep temperatures on the \ncool side widespread today into tonight. Highs will be mainly in\nthe 60s. Any rain chances should come to an end around this \nevening. Overnight conditions should be similar to last night \nwith lows again in the 50s.\n\nAs a deep upper trough tracks across far eastern Canada, a more\npotent cold front sweeps through the northeastern U.S. on\nMonday. This will favor increasing sunshine, but also a large\nuptick in northwesterly winds. Afternoon gusts up to 20 to 25\nmph are possible, but with highs returning to the upper 60s to\nmid 70s (upper 50s to 60s in the mountains).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freezing temperatures are possible to\nthe west of the Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nA strong area of high pressure will build over the area next \nweek, leading to an extended period of dry conditions. As a cooler \nairmass works in and high pressure builds overhead, frost and \nfreezing temperatures may be possible to the west of the Blue Ridge \nboth Monday night and Tuesday night. We'll start to see slightly \nwarmer air build in from the south and west later in the week. High \ntemperatures at lower elevations are forecast to be in the 60s \n(upper 50s/low 60s mountains) on Tuesday, upper 60s to low 70s on \nWednesday (60s mountains), and mid 70s (mid-upper 60s mountains) on \nWednesday and Thursday. Low temperatures will gradually moderate \nfrom the 30s/40s early in the week, to the upper 40s/low 50s later \nin the week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /13Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nLower ceilings and visibility through mid-morning as drizzle,\npatchy fog, and pockets of light rain encompass much of the\neastern two-thirds of the region. MVFR to IFR conditions would\nbe the flavor.\n\nRain likely continues through the first half of today before a \ndrying trend before dark. Low ceilings likely persist into\ntonight given persistent northeasterly flow. However, VFR \nconditions return for Monday behind a strong cold front. Gusts \nup to 20 to 25 knots are possible during the afternoon hours.\n\nVFR conditions are expected on both Tuesday and Wednesday. \nWinds are forecast to be out of the north on Tuesday, and then \nsouth on Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nGusts up to around 10 to 15 knots are expected across the\nChesapeake Bay this morning before diminishing a bit later in\nthe day. Prevailing winds will mainly be out of the north to\nnortheasterly direction given the CAD wedge in place. The true \nramp up in wind fields comes for the second half of Monday \nbehind a cold front. Small Craft Advisories will likely be \nneeded midday Monday through the night.\n\nSub-SCA level northerly winds are expected on Tuesday. Wind shift \nto out of the south on Wednesday, and are expected to remain sub-SCA \nin nature.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies have risen quite a bit over the last 6 to 12\nhours with current values around 1 to 1.25 feet. This is\nbringing some of the more sensitive locations into Action stage.\nThe forecast ahead shows anomalies remaining elevated through\nearly portions of the week. However, do expect some drop off\nwith the shift to northwesterly winds behind Monday's cold\nfrontal passage.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/KLW/KJP\nAVIATION...BRO/KLW/KJP\nMARINE...BRO/KLW/KJP\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...BRO\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791127267,"days":{"2026-10-04":{"hi":67,"lo":58,"pop":47,"short":"Areas Of Drizzle"},"2026-10-05":{"hi":71,"lo":48,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-06":{"hi":66,"lo":47,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-07":{"hi":71,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":75,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-09":{"hi":74,"lo":55,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":70,"lo":54,"pop":5,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791126000,"n":48,"ceil":[600,500,500,500,500,500,400,400,500,600,800,900,1100,2100,3000,4000,4700,5300,6000,10900,19900,24800,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[7.0,7.0,7.0,7.0,7.